// GENERATED by smart-base/L1/schemas/emit.ts from smart-base/L2/schemas/l2.ts (L2 3.0). Do not edit: change the Zod source and run `npm run build` in smart-base/L1/schemas/.
CodeSystem: KGL2Predicates
Title: "Knowledge graph L2: predicates"
Description: "The predicates of the L2 layer. An edge's `predicate` is one of these; which (source, target) pairs each licenses is the layer's edge table. Source: smart-base/L1/schemas/L2.ts (migrated from smart-kg ontology/L2/L2.json)."

* ^experimental = false
* ^caseSensitive = true
* ^status = #active
* ^publisher = "World Health Organization (WHO)"

* #hasComponent "hasComponent" "A DAK contains one of its nine components. The qualifier carries the component name from DAK.fsh, so `dak hasComponent persona` and `dak hasComponent program-indicator` stay distinguishable when a query walks the kit."
* #sourcedFrom "sourcedFrom" "A component points at the file that defines it. TWO TARGETS ARE LICENSED, and which one an instance uses says how much is known about the file. Where a subgraph models that file type, the target is the file itself -- `business-process sourcedFrom bpmn-definitions`, licensed in l2-bpmn -- and the graph walks straight on into the file's contents. Where none does (a Gherkin feature, a FHIR canonical, or a BPMN this graph simply does not hold), the target is an l1:external-artifact carrying a URI and nothing else. Prefer the first: routing through external-artifact when the file IS modelled adds a hop through a node that, at instance level, is the same node."
* #realises "realises" "A business process realises a user scenario. From `scenario 0..* id` on BusinessProcessWorkflow -- a bare id string, so an instance carries a resolutionStatus."
* #involves "involves" "A user scenario involves a persona. From `personas 0..* id` on UserScenario -- again bare ids, again a resolutionStatus."
* #fulfilledBy "fulfilledBy" "A functional requirement is fulfilled by a persona. THE ONE JOIN IN THIS LAYER THAT IS NOT STRING MATCHING: the committed instances write `actor[+] = Canonical(SGAuthoring.Persona.BusinessAnalyst)`, a canonical reference that resolves without guessing. It is the counter-example that shows the other joins are a choice, not a necessity."
* #hasStatement "hasStatement" "A requirement carries numbered, labelled statements with a conformance verb. 170 of them exist today."
* #listsTask "listsTask" "BusinessProcessWorkflow declares `task 0..*` with identifier, name and description -- the same tasks the BPMN file draws. Two representations of one list, joined by identifier, which is why dak-task exists as a node rather than a property."
* #references "references" "A program indicator names the health interventions that give it context. From `references 0..* id` on ProgramIndicator -- bare id strings again, so an instance carries a resolutionStatus. Distinct from derivedFrom: referencing an intervention for context is not the same claim as restating a normative indicator."
* #describes "describes" "A DAK-level task entry describes an element of a BPMN file. Declared here and licensed in l2-bpmn, where the target class lives."
* #implementedBy "implementedBy" "Reused from l1, and licensed here for the L1->L2 hop that L1 could not declare itself: l1 cannot name an l2 class without a circular import, but l2 imports l1 and sees both ends. A recommendation is implemented by the DAK component that realises it. Always `inferred`, never `derived` -- deciding that a process realises a recommendation is adaptation, the judgement a DAK exists to record, and it carries a resolutionStatus like every other cross-artefact claim. Note health-intervention has NO implementedBy edge: the published SOP gives Health Interventions an L3 row of `n/a / n/a / n/a` and calls L1 recommendations inputs rather than outputs, so the trace runs through processes, decisions and indicators instead."
* #derivedFrom "derivedFrom" "This normative content restates or operationalises that recommendation."
* #appearsIn "appearsIn" "A citation string appears in an L2 or L3 artefact at a stated location."
* #verifiedBy "verifiedBy" "A requirement statement is verified by a test scenario. The statement is the granularity at which conformance is asserted, so it is also the granularity at which a test is claimed to check it. The edge records the claim that a scenario exists for the statement; whether the scenario actually exercises each success criterion is a review question, recorded per criterion by `verification` on successCriteria, not by this edge."
